//
// PROBE: F18's fourteen — the words whose depth guard under-declares.
//
// Each guards `< 1` in the reference and then pulls **two**
// (reference/rust_multistackvm/src/stdlib/values/value_carcdr.rs:27-33,64-67
// for the list words; artefacts.rs:7,10,16 for `pair`), so one operand short
// gives `NO DATA #2` on a stack the first pull has already emptied. F18's
// disposition is FIX: declare the arity the word consumes, so the guard fires
// before anything is taken and the operand survives. That deviation is not
// visible here — it is pinned by test, because a golden of it would capture
// the reference's `~/.cargo` path (F66).
//
// What *is* pinned here is that the words agree on every case that works, and
// three orders that are easy to get backwards and were:
//
//   - `head`/`tail`/`at` take the **list on top**, count beneath
//     (`value_carcdr.rs:27-33` pulls the value before the arm pulls its count).
//   - `string.regex` and `string.wildcard` take the **subject on top**,
//     pattern beneath (`regex.rs:28,31`) — the opposite of
//     `string.expressionmatch`, whose expression is on top.
//   - `at` is **one-based** and refuses 0 (`carcdr.rs:57-60`).
//
// Two answers are refusals rather than values: `complex` builds a `CFLOAT` and
// printing one reports `Can not convert Value from 15`, exactly as a PAIR
// reports 10. Both are the tag/payload split — a numeric payload that no
// conversion arm admits.
//
2 [ 1 2 3 4 ] head println
2 [ 1 2 3 4 ] tail println
2 [ 1 2 3 4 ] at println
1 42 head println
1.0 2.0 complex drop
"hello world" "h.*d" string.regex println
"hello" "z.*" string.regex println
"hello.txt" "*.txt" string.wildcard println
"hello.txt" "*.md" string.wildcard println
"kitten" "sitting" string.distance.sift3 println
"kitten" "sitting" string.distance.jarowinkler println
"abc" "xyz" string.distance.hamming println
